\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{frap}
\title{Further connections}

\p{Orientation arithmetic leads beyond modular sums. Recording both positions
and orientations produces wreath products. Their abelianized characters detect
global obstructions, and finite-index transfer explains the same cancellation
through coset representatives. The local rotation groups change with abstract
dimension: an alternating group can have a nontrivial abelianization in small
dimensions and a trivial one in larger dimensions. A vanished character means
that this particular detector has lost information; it does not make every
configuration reachable \citet{Section 3.1}{marchetti2025rubik}.}

\p{Transport offers a separate geometric direction. A sequence of moves may
return pieces to their positions while changing their orientations. Transporting
labels between neighboring simplices is another construction, studied through
projectivities, coloring obstructions, and branched coverings. For example,
the complete unfolding of the tetrahedron boundary is a torus. These phenomena
motivate a linked study of transport and topology; identifying the two kinds of
transport would require additional arguments.
[Izmestiev and Joswig](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0108202) develop the geometric
construction. It is not a prerequisite for the route through layered cubes.}

\p{Random moves raise a different question from solving: when, and how quickly,
does a scramble lose information about its starting state? A random walk depends
on its allowed moves and their probabilities. Functions on the move group can
measure persistent information; representation theory organizes these functions
and the action of averaging over moves. Related Fourier coordinates can describe
functions used to guide search, as in
[Pan and Kondor](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v130/pan21a.html).
A useful learned search score is not automatically a lower bound on solution
length, just as a long random sequence is not automatically a uniform scramble.}
